  "description": "Compute the element-wise Hadamard product of two same-shaped matrices.\n\nHadamard products implement gating mechanisms (such as in LSTM cells and GRU units) and feature masking.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nC_{ij} = (A \\odot B)_{ij} = A_{ij} B_{ij}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Verify identical shape dimensions across input matrices\n2. Execute element-wise multiplication between corresponding index entries\n3. Return resulting Hadamard product array\n\n### Returns\nNested list of float values.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Hadamard Product\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **Hadamard Product** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nHadamard products implement gating mechanisms (such as in LSTM cells and GRU units) and feature masking. In this problem, the goal is to: compute the element-wise hadamard product of two same-shaped matrices.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nC_{ij} = (A \\odot B)_{ij} = A_{ij} B_{ij}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Verify identical shape dimensions across input matrices\n2. **Phase 2**: Execute element-wise multiplication between corresponding index entries\n3. **Phase 3**: Return resulting Hadamard product array\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: matrix1 = [[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]], matrix2 = [[5.0, 6.0], [7.0, 8.0]]\n\nOutput: [[5.0, 12.0], [21.0, 32.0]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated la_hadamard_product under standard test conditions."
  ],
